edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186192802 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186193178 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186192802 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186193205 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186192802 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186278568 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186192802 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186278570 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186192802 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186378836 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186192802 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186378843 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186192802 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186398541 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186192802 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186398548 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186192802 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186398555 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186192802 #edb/ref 1039 "Identifying the Boundary of a Set" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186192802 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186192530 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186192802 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "e534c006-a5b4-5f76-bbd6-451496358d3a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186192917 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186193662 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186192917 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186193663 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186192917 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186193664 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186192917 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186193696 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186192917 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186192917 #edb/ref 1045 "Interiors and Boundaries of Sets" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186192917 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186193663 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186192917 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186048621 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186192917 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "d93075cf-1282-5b7f-b71d-4c5f5fd85e47" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193206 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186278077 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193206 #edb/ref 1069 "q-161444" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193206 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193206 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the set $A,$ defined by \n\n$$\nA=\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,4{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\ge 4\\}.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $\\text{int}\\,A\\cap ∂A=∅$\n\nII. $A-∂A=\\text{int}\\,A$\n\nIII. $∂A⊂A$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193206 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "68256c62-7faf-5747-9e8c-b2c67a0a95a2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193206 #edb/ref 1074 "The boundary of a set $A,$ denoted $∂A,$ is the set that contains all the boundary points of $A.$\n\nIn this case, the set $A$ and its boundary $∂A$ are shown below.\n\n![](images/62/62c410c21e79e1bc688c1ff1a3440a207c62bcad42525bdc93fa2770b76d48bd.png)\n\nThe interior of a set $A,$ denoted $\\text{int}\\,A,$ is the set containing all the interior points of $A.$ It contains every point in $A$ except the boundary points: \n\n$$\n\\text{int}\\,A=A-∂A\n$$\n\nThe interior of $A$ is shown below.\n\n![](images/33/33a4738b642443c2535d347aa53ee2b7b80c800142c4844d94578769118ebfbc.png)\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement:\n\n- Statement I is true. Since $\\text{int}\\,A$ contains all points in $A$ except the boundary points, we must have $\\text{int}\\,A\\cap ∂A=∅,$ where $∅$ denotes the empty set.\n\n- Statement II is true. Since $\\text{int}\\,A$ contains all points in $A$ except the boundary points, we must have $A-∂A=\\text{int}\\,A.$\n\n- Statement III is true. In this case, the set $A$ contains all of its boundary points, so we must have $∂A⊂A.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193662 #edb/ref 1061 41922 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193662 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "a9a56a41-bd42-55bc-97e0-3044e441a107" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193662 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186192786 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193663 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186193696 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193663 #edb/ref 1061 41923 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193663 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "a6d2c5fc-ab0d-5230-b12f-968073fd01a2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193663 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186193885 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193664 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186193662 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193664 #edb/ref 1061 41924 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193664 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "9d5d8127-b825-594a-81df-e04296e04e38" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193664 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186192787 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193696 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186193664 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193696 #edb/ref 1061 44507 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193696 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "96d5ce65-6ad2-5f74-bcdb-8d90d0ff8464" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193696 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186192802 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193885 #edb/ref 1064 "The **boundary** of a set $S,$ denoted $\\partial S,$ is the set that contains all the boundary points of $S.$\n\nFor example, consider the set $A,$ given by \n\n$$\nA = \\big\\{ (x,y)\\in\\mathbb R^2 \\,:\\, -1 \\lt x \\leq 1, \\, 0 \\lt y \\leq 2 \\big\\}.\n$$\n\nWe can represent this set as the following shaded region.\n\n![](images/50/5033928a09f9c320973a223bfd49b4b67924b433c7ba53e58bc07a95905f4231.png)\n\nThen, the set $\\partial A,$ the boundary of $A,$ has the following representation in the plane.\n\n![](images/e2/e2034b17b6a6834c1d747e2eba4a2522c43e9ff888d5f7feb7bf716b3e2cec00.png)\n\nThe **interior** of a set $S,$ denoted $\\textrm{int}\\, S,$ is the set containing all the interior points of $S.$ It contains every point in $S$ except the boundary points: \n\n$$\n\\textrm{int}\\,S = S - \\partial S\n$$\n\nFor our set $A$ defined above, the interior looks as follows:\n\n![](images/57/57ab8735d761926a36aa0e469b4637482c31880cc446d913f93755a12ec65a8f.png)\n\nMore formally, the interior of $A$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\textrm{int}\\,A = \\big\\{ (x,y)\\in\\mathbb R^2 \\,:\\, -1 \\lt x \\lt 1, \\, 0 \\lt y \\lt 2 \\big\\}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193885 #edb/ref 1065 6925 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [1