e-a249-5090-bfed-df0660f7b0eb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186278571 #edb/ref 1074 "The boundary of a set $A,$ denoted $∂A,$ is the set that contains all the boundary points of $A.$\n\nIn this case, the set $A$ and its boundary $∂A$ are shown below.\n\n![](images/ff/ff1f735266f66879834e02ca7601e90e40dfa7929e5796d4a3f8cb4ff2828de7.png)\n\n  \n\nThe interior of a set $A,$ denoted $\\text{int}\\,A,$ is the set containing all the interior points of $A.$ It contains every point in $A$ except the boundary points: \n\n$$\n\\text{int}\\,A=A-∂A\n$$\n\nNotice that, in this case, the set $A$ contains none of its boundary points. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement:\n\n- Statement I is true, while statement II is false. As we've seen, $A$ contains none of its boundary points.\n\n- Statement III is true. Since $A$ contains none of its boundary points, we must have $\\text{int}\\,A=A.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186278638 #edb/ref 1080 "III  only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186278638 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186278638 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "052b75ab-358d-5073-b438-8aabbcf16598" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186278766 #edb/ref 1080 "4{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}<4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186278766 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186278766 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "1325c53d-2a90-5778-afb8-06fcd3205174" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186278794 #edb/ref 1080 "0\\le x<2,\\text{ }0\\le y<2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186278794 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186278794 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "16ed68dd-7aef-5656-ac3a-0cafe1572795" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186278896 #edb/ref 1080 "{x}^{2}-1\\le y\\le 0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186278896 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186278896 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "21a437c9-898a-560e-9410-f1b54bde1b49" #edb/ref 175921